# Compact build output. Regenerate with `npm run compact`.
contracts/managed/
contracts/managed-vault/
# The symlink `compile-vault.sh` makes so the vault's generated caller can find
# the account's generated module at the path it imports. Build output pointing
# at build output.
contracts/Acct
contracts/build/

# Network state: wallet seeds and deployed contract addresses.
# The seed file controls real test funds on preview. It is not secret in the
# sense that money is at stake, but losing it means asking the faucet again,
# and committing it means anyone who reads the repo can spend those funds.
.midnight/

# THE MONEY-LOSS REGISTER. Internal only, decided 16 Aug.
#
# It names, precisely, every way a customer could currently lose access to their
# funds and which of them are still open. That is exactly the document to keep
# honest and exactly the wrong one to publish while the gaps are open — a public
# version gets written deliberately when this repo is pushed (S-13).
#
# NOT BEING IN GIT MEANS IT IS NOT BACKED UP BY GIT. Its backup is a copy kept
# outside this repository; the file here is the source and that copy is
# refreshed whenever it changes.
docs/how-money-can-be-lost.md

# Private working documents. Never published.
# BACKLOG.md is the live gap list: it churns constantly and contains honest
# admissions about what is unfinished, which belong to us and not to a repo
# anyone else may read. Keep it on disk, keep it out of git.
BACKLOG.md
NOTES.md
scratch/
REPORT.txt
REPORT-PROVING.txt
REPORT-PREFLIGHT.txt
REPORT-DEPLOY.txt

# Everything the .command files leave behind. The list had drifted: reports,
# stack samples and symbol maps were all landing in git's view (M-40).
REPORT-*.txt
logs/
.wallet-state/

# The private state store. Contract-address-keyed, rebuilt on demand, and it
# holds signer secrets in an encrypted LevelDB. Never in git.
midnight-level-db/

# Scratch left behind where `rm` is unavailable, and pre-reset archives.
# Both are cleared out by hand; neither belongs in history.
_to_delete/
.pre-reset-*/
.fuse_hidden*

# Probe output. These are throwaway compiler runs from experiments — the same
# kind of build artifact as contracts/managed/, which is ignored two rules up,
# and they carry proving and verifier keys measured in megabytes.
#
# THREE OF THESE DIRECTORIES WERE COMMITTED on 16 Aug before this rule existed,
# so removing them from the tree does not remove them from the history. That is
# recorded in S-13, which is the pre-push checklist.
contracts/probe-out*/
contracts/probe/
contracts/probe4/
contracts/probe5/

# The commit message COMMIT.command reads. It is regenerated for every commit,
# so tracking it would put the previous message inside the next commit — the
# message about the message.
COMMIT-MESSAGE.md

# The file list COMMIT.command stages from. Same shape and the same reason: it
# is the round's own account of what it wrote, rewritten whole for each commit,
# so tracking it would put the last round's list inside this round's commit.
COMMIT-FILES.txt

# The record PUBLIC-REPO-APPLY.command writes when it has run. It describes one
# machine at one moment, like a report.
PUBLIC-REPO-APPLIED.txt

# Identity is its own repository. Nothing under here belongs to this one.
/Identity/

# REMOVED 5 Sep 2026. `src/testing/no-attribution.test.ts` IS TRACKED.
#
# The rule that used to sit here excluded it, and the reason it gave had
# stopped being true: it said the test "reads files from `Identity` as well as
# from here", so a clone of this repository alone could not run it. The test no
# longer walks `Identity/` — its own header records that change, and its only
# directory reads are rooted at this repository. Measured, not assumed.
#
# IT IS THE ONLY MECHANICAL ENFORCEMENT OF THE RULE THAT NO FILE ATTRIBUTES
# WORK TO ANYTHING BUT A PERSON. Excluding it left that rule enforced by
# nothing, in the repository where it finally matters.
#
# WHAT IS STILL OPEN, AND IT IS TRACKED RATHER THAN LEFT AS A SURPRISE: the
# test asserts it found more than thirty `.command` files at the root. Those
# are on disk here, so it passes; a fresh clone has none, so it will fail there
# until the check is repointed at the tracked tree. That is owed BEFORE CI, and
# CI does not exist yet.

# A working list, like the register and the backlog. Not for the public repo.
docs/cleanup-before-github.md

# The Foundation's source, cloned in so this folder is self-contained. Never pushed.
midnight-src/

# An exploration written FOR THE CONTROLLER, not for the repository. It scopes a
# possible integration with an external product and reasons about liquidity and
# custody arrangements that have not been decided. It is a report, not a plan, and
# it is not part of the software.
docs/scope-safe-plugin.md

# SAFE's source, cloned for reading by CLONE-SAFE-SRC.command. Same reason as
# midnight-src/: a scope that cites source beats one that cites memory.
SAFE/
REPORT-CLONE-SAFE.txt

# Key snapshots taken by BACKUP-KEYS.command. Private keys and chain records,
# on the same disk as the originals, deliberately reachable by sessions and
# .command files. Never in git.
.keys-backup/

# ROUND BRIEFS AND CONTROLLER WORKING FILES. Added 29 Aug.
#
# A brief is an instruction to one session. It is a working document with the
# same character as BACKLOG.md and the register, both ignored above, and it has
# no reader once the round it describes is finished. Tracking them put working
# notes and product decisions into commits that carried nothing else, which is
# the kind of history this repository has already paid to clean up once.
#
# THE BRIEFS ALREADY TRACKED ARE NOT REMOVED BY THIS RULE. Ignoring a tracked
# file does nothing; they stay in the index and in the history until something
# removes them, and that is `docs/cleanup-before-github.md`'s job, not this
# rule's. What this rule does is stop the next one entering history at all.
docs/ROUND-*.md
docs/HOLD-*.md

# CLAUDE.md. The operating manual for the sessions that build this.
#
# It names nobody, but it describes how work is done here rather than what the
# software is: rounds, briefs, the register, the backlog, and the scripts a
# person double-clicks. That is internal process, and it is the same class of
# detail that is already kept out of commit messages.
#
# It costs nothing to ignore. Sessions read it from the working tree, never from
# a clone, so a repository without it builds and tests exactly the same.
#
# ALREADY TRACKED. Ignoring it stops further changes entering history; it does
# not remove what is already there. That is `docs/cleanup-before-github.md`.
CLAUDE.md

# THE CONVERTER DESIGN DOCUMENTS. Added 29 Aug.
#
# These describe a contract that has not been written. They carry custody
# reasoning, the question that belongs to a lawyer, the governance of a
# committee that does not exist, and product decisions that are still being
# taken. That is design material, not software, and this repository publishes.
#
# ALREADY TRACKED, AND NOT REMOVED BY THIS RULE: docs/scope-the-converter.md and
# docs/converter-research.md. Ignoring a tracked file does nothing; removing
# them is docs/cleanup-before-github.md's job.
docs/converter.md
docs/converter-immutable.md
docs/converter-upgradable.md

# WHERE WE DIFFER FROM THE FOUNDATION'S CODE. Added 29 Aug. P0 document.
#
# It lists, with file and line, every place this project contradicts the
# Foundation's code or its comments, claims a gap or a bug in it, works around a
# behaviour, or declines to use a module it provides.
#
# IGNORED BY DEFAULT AND THIS IS A DECISION THAT CAN BE OVERTURNED. Publishing a
# list of suspected defects in somebody else's audited code, from our own
# repository, is not the same act as reporting them to the people who wrote it.
# The document exists so those reports can be made accurately; where they are
# made is a separate choice.
docs/foundation-divergences.md

# The controller briefing pack for the contract-stack scope. A working input, not software.
docs/contract-stack-briefing.md

# The corrections ledger. Internal quality data; it names roles and rounds.
docs/corrections.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTROLLER'S FILES. Added 31 Aug 2026.
#
# docs/BOARD.md is the master build list. It carries round names, defect ids,
# the founder's own words and the order of unreleased work, and it has exactly
# the character of BACKLOG.md and the register, both ignored above.
#
# THREE OF THE FILES NAMED HERE ARE ALREADY TRACKED, AND IGNORING A TRACKED
# FILE DOES NOTHING: docs/BOARD.md, docs/build-log.md and
# docs/handoff/05-why-the-rules.md. Removing them from the index is `git rm
# --cached`, which no session may run — it is a person's step, or it is settled
# by the push gate's item 4, the fresh `git init`. These lines are here so that
# a fresh init honours them from its first commit.
#
# docs/HOLD-*.md and docs/ROUND-*.md are already covered above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/BOARD.md
docs/build-log.md
docs/handoff/05-why-the-rules.md

# THE BUILD ORDER LIST. Added 31 Aug 2026, extracted out of docs/BOARD.md.
#
# Same character as docs/BOARD.md, ignored above: round names, defect ids, the
# founder's own words and the order of unreleased work. It is CONTROLLER-ONLY
# under rule 32 and it is ignored so that it can be edited while a build session
# is running in this tree without either one touching the other.
docs/BUILD-ORDER.md

# The design doc set PLAN, not the set itself. Controller-only, like docs/BUILD-ORDER.md:
# it is edited while sessions run and no round writes it. The SET it plans (docs/design/*.md)
# IS tracked and does go to GitHub - this planning file does not.
docs/the-doc-set.md

# ROUND HANDOFF DOCUMENTS. Added 5 Sep 2026.
#
# A handoff doc is what a round writes INSTEAD OF writing the shared files, when
# it is running in parallel with another round in this same working tree. It
# carries the round's findings in full - claim, file:line, measurement, proposed
# grade, done-when, proposed board row - and the CONTROLLER transcribes them into
# BACKLOG.md, the register, docs/BUILD-ORDER.md and the build log afterwards.
#
# WHY IGNORED, AND IT IS THE SAME REASON docs/BUILD-ORDER.md IS: COMMIT.command
# runs `git add -A`, so anything tracked in this tree lands in whichever round
# commits next, under a message describing the other round's work. Ignoring it is
# what makes two rounds in one tree safe rather than merely lucky.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS: rule 16 - a row cited and not yet written. That is a real loss
# and it has been paid here before (T-311). Until CHECK-BOARD.command carries the
# check that refuses an untranscribed handoff, the only thing holding it is the
# controller, and a handoff is transcribed BEFORE the next round is prompted.
#
# THE ROUND NEVER ASSIGNS T- OR C- IDS. It labels findings F1, F2, F3. Two rounds
# writing rows at once would both start at the same next id, in a file with no git
# history, and nothing would ever say so.
docs/HANDOFF-*.md

# THE AGENT DEFINITIONS. Added 5 Sep 2026.
#
# `.claude/agents/*.md` define the subagents rounds spawn — the
# `money-safety-auditor`, the `platform-fact-checker`, the `test-auditor`. They
# describe HOW work is checked here rather than WHAT the software does, which is
# the same class as CLAUDE.md two rules up, and the same reason.
#
# FOUND 5 Sep WHILE ANSWERING WHETHER A FRESH REPOSITORY WOULD START CLEAN. Every
# other working file was already ignored; this one was not, and `.claude/` is
# listed in docs/cleanup-before-github.md §A as tracked in this repository.
# Ignoring it now means a fresh init excludes it from the first commit.
.claude/
